A||||5||My7Fieros||Why does the tach in my 84 always show 1500rpm while off?||||5||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||12-09-2001||04:17 PM||||Ive noticed this on several 4 cyl models, but the tach in my Indy sits at around 1500 rpms....when the car isnt even running! Whats the deal? Is there a way to correct this prob? It doesnt do that on my V6s....curious, thanx.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||12-09-2001||04:41 PM||||Just the nature of the beast.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ferrari_cdn||12-09-2001||04:54 PM||||Thats how my car works too.  When I turn off the motor from idle (at 900), it will jump to 1500 and stay there.  But when you turn the ignition key but NOT start the car, it will go down to 0.  Turn it off and it'll stay there.<P>I guess thats how 84s work.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000003||watts||12-09-2001||06:54 PM||||It's because of the type of meter movement.  If you took off the 'glass', you could put the needle anywhere you like and it'll stay there until the driver circuit is fired up (unlike a multi meter for example).  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-09-2001||07:12 PM||||its magnetic, when you shut it off it just goes where it wants to, till you put power back on.||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||12-09-2001||11:02 PM||||Ahhh, ok. thanx.||||reg||14
